    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "retenir",
     "title": "Looking after soil",
     "md": "Farmers improve soil with **manure** and **compost** (rotted plant waste). **Crop rotation** means growing different crops in turn, such as maize then beans. **Fallow** means letting land rest. **Mulching** covers the soil with leaves. **Terraces** on hills and planting **across** a slope slow the water. Chemical fertilisers can help, but they must be used carefully."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- Burning the land every year: it kills the small animals and removes humus.\n- Planting the same crop on the same land for years.\n- Ploughing straight down a slope.\n- Thinking that fertiliser is a replacement for taking care of the soil."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1",
     "statement": "Why do farmers grow beans after maize on the same field?",
     "steps": [
      "Different crops use different minerals; beans also help to put nitrogen back in the soil.",
      "This is crop rotation, and it keeps the soil fertile."
     ],
     "answer": "Crop rotation keeps the soil fertile."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 2",
     "statement": "A farmer in the Bamenda highlands ploughs down the slope and soil is washed away. What should he do?",
     "steps": [
      "He should plant across the slope or make terraces.",
      "He can also plant grass and trees on the steepest parts."
     ],
     "answer": "Plant across the slope or build terraces, and cover the soil."
    }
